# 5. Experimental Evaluation Metrics & Infrastructure

## 5.1 Causal Funnel Progression Metrics

HEIST evaluates policies along the sequential causal chain. We measure five rates.

1. Scout Tag Rate ($ P_{\text{tag}} $): $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{len}(\text{tagged\_pois}) > 0) \right] $
2. Terminal Disable Rate ($ P_{\text{term}} $): $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{terminal\_disabled}) \right] $
3. Loot Acquisition Rate ($ P_{\text{loot}} $): $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{loot\_acquired}) \right] $
4. Extraction Call Rate ($ P_{\text{ext}} $): $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{extraction\_triggered}) \right] $
5. Team Win Rate ($ P_{\text{win}} $): $ \mathbb{E} \left[ \mathbb{I}(\text{win}) \right] $

A model suffers from causal bottlenecking at stage $ m $ if $ P_m > 0 $ but $ P_{m+1} \approx 0 $.

## 5.2 Credit Attribution Index (CAI)

We define the Credit Attribution Index (CAI) for agent $ i $. It quantifies how an algorithm attributes credit to upstream enablers versus downstream extractors:

$ \text{CAI}_i = \text{Corr}\left( \sum_{t=1}^T r_{t,i}^{\text{shaped}}, \quad Y_{\text{terminal}} \right) $

The term $ Y_{\text{terminal}} \in \{+15, -10\} $ is the episode's shared outcome. High positive $ \text{CAI}_i $ for Scout and Hacker indicates that enabling contributions correlate with team success.

## 5.3 Loss Mode Cause Attribution

Episode failures are classified into three disjoint loss modes. We use multiline TeX case math:

$$$
\text{Loss Cause} =
\begin{cases}
    \text{alarm\_max} & \text{if } A_t \ge 100.0 \\
    \text{guard\_catch} & \text{if } \exists i, j: \text{Manhattan}(p_i, g_j) \le d_{\text{catch}} \\
    \text{timeout} & \text{if } t \ge T_{\text{max}}
\end{cases}
$$$

## 5.4 Hardware Protection Kill Switch (`tools/thermal_guard.py`)

HEIST executes live thermal monitoring at every iteration. It prevents thermal degradation during long multi-stage campaign runs.

The CPU threshold is $ T_{\text{CPU}} < 85.0^\circ\text{C} $. The GPU threshold is $ T_{\text{GPU}} < 83.0^\circ\text{C} $.

If temperature exceeds limits, `thermal_guard.py` triggers an emergency `pkill`. This stops python processes and protects hardware integrity.

## 5.5 Experimental Results: The Sparsity Wall

We empirically evaluated flat MARL models across the HEIST curriculum. MAPPO, COMA, MAPPO-CAR, and MARC learn on dense maps. They hit a Sparsity Wall in large sparse maps (Stage 4).

The time-delay between causal dependencies is large. Flat Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) signal decays to zero before upstream enabling actions receive credit. Value gradients collapse. The models fail to explore. They achieve a 0.000 win rate across all baselines in Stage 4.

